Day 20 | AWS Load Balancing, Auto Scaling & High Availability

Overview

Studied the architecture and core concepts behind building resilient, scalable, and highly available applications on AWS.

Key Technical Learnings

1. Application Load Balancer (ALB)

- Explored Layer 7 load balancing for HTTP/HTTPS traffic.
- Studied Target Groups, target health checks, and traffic distribution.
- Understood how ALB routes requests to healthy registered targets.
- Distinguished traffic management from EC2 instance provisioning and recovery.

2. EC2 Launch Templates

- Studied how Launch Templates define reusable EC2 launch configurations.
- Reviewed key parameters, including AMI, instance type, security groups, and storage.
- Understood how Launch Templates support consistent instance provisioning through Auto Scaling Groups.

3. EC2 Auto Scaling

- Learned the purpose of minimum, desired, and maximum capacity.
- Explored how Auto Scaling Groups maintain configured capacity and replace unhealthy instances when health monitoring and replacement settings are configured appropriately.
- Understood the relationship between Auto Scaling Groups and Launch Templates.

4. Availability Zones & High Availability

- Studied the relationship between AWS Regions and Availability Zones.
- Explored multi-AZ deployment as a strategy for improving application resilience.
- Understood how distributing compute resources across AZs can reduce the impact of an individual AZ failure.
- Reviewed the importance of health checks, traffic routing, application design, and data availability in achieving high availability.

Architecture Understanding

Request Flow: Users → Application Load Balancer → Target Group → Healthy EC2 Instances

Scaling Workflow: Auto Scaling Group → Launch Template → EC2 Instance Provisioning

High Availability Strategy: Distribute application resources across multiple Availability Zones and configure health monitoring, routing, and recovery mechanisms.

Hands-on & Console Exploration

- Inspected the AWS console sections for Load Balancers, Target Groups, Auto Scaling Groups, Launch Templates, EC2 instances, and route tables.
- Reviewed how the core components fit together in a scalable AWS architecture.
- Used a cost-conscious, inspection-first approach without creating additional chargeable resources.

Cloud Practitioner Exam Preparation

- Completed 5 practice MCQs with a score of 5/5 (100%).
- Revised ALB, Auto Scaling, desired capacity, health checks, Availability Zones, and High Availability.

Key Takeaway

Developed a clearer understanding of how AWS load balancing, automated compute capacity management, reusable instance configurations, and multi-AZ architecture work together to support scalable and resilient applications.

